The dictionary of a file
29c-sd-tcl-the-development-tools
Name a file after the verb (tool.dict customers) or pick one from the list with Enter. The items pane lists every item of its dictionary: the id, the type (D data, A and S in the Pick layout, I computed, X text), where the value comes from, the heading, format, conversion, whether it is single or multivalued, the association, and the expression of a computed item. The box under the list says in words what the item under the cursor shows and how it is printed.
N makes an item: it asks the id and the type, then the fields that type needs, one at a time, and checks each answer. The box shows the whole record and says what it will do. After Run the page writes the record itself, as an ordinary record write - the only thing it writes - and only if you hold dict-edit; if you do not, the box says so and the write is refused with the kernel's own words. E edits the item in SED (the terminal is handed to SED with no box; Enter brings the page back). D deletes it (delete dict) after its id is typed. C compiles an A, S or I item (compile.dict). D and C are the command you could type, shown in a box first; Cancel is what Enter chooses. N's box shows the record the page writes, which no verb takes. An id or an index name that SD reads as a keyword (all, no.query, detail ...) is not given to a verb: the page says which command to type.
T tries an item on a record of the file: it shows the value the query processor would compute for that record, and the same value converted and formatted as a query would print it. An item that needs compiling must be compiled first. The page writes nothing, but an item that calls a subroutine of the account runs that subroutine, as a query would.
I or Tab shows the alternate key indices of the file. N makes one on an item you pick from the list (create.index defines it and then build.index builds it, because an index that is not built finds nothing), B builds it again from the records (build.index), D deletes it after its name is typed (delete.index) and L lists it in full (list.index, which prints on the terminal; Enter brings the page back). Build and delete need the file to themselves: if another session has it open the verb says so and nothing changes.
An id or an index name with a capital, a dollar or an at sign is listed and described, but the page does not give it to a command; it tells you which verb to type. / or F11 filters the ids; Esc clears the filter; F5 reads the dictionary again; F goes back to the list of files.
